1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,321
BlockID Business Passport, on Monad.

2
00:00:03,321 --> 00:00:07,755
Built for Track 4: trust, identity and AI infrastructure.

3
00:00:08,622 --> 00:00:12,725
If you own two percent of a private company, today you own a PDF.

4
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:22,820
Business Passport gives every shareholder a live share register on Monad, monthly updates drafted by AI and signed off by a director, and dividends paid to every wallet in one transaction.

5
00:00:23,678 --> 00:00:25,199
Here is the flow.

6
00:00:25,199 --> 00:00:27,121
The company connects its data.

7
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:29,586
Our AI pipeline drafts the update.

8
00:00:29,586 --> 00:00:32,097
The issuer service records it on Monad.

9
00:00:32,097 --> 00:00:35,989
A director approves it from a separate wallet, or a passkey smart account.

10
00:00:35,989 --> 00:00:39,291
Only then does Monad anchor the update and pay the holders.

11
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:43,499
This is the update the AI drafted for a demo company.

12
00:00:43,499 --> 00:00:48,369
Every claim is marked evidenced or missing, with an evidence confidence of eighty-two percent.

13
00:00:48,369 --> 00:00:52,626
Its exact bytes are hashed and recorded on Monad, but it is not final yet.

14
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:54,633
Step two.

15
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:57,180
The director approves on Monad testnet.

16
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:02,027
This transaction comes from the director's wallet, not from the service that recorded the draft.

17
00:01:02,027 --> 00:01:07,982
In fact, the contract never lets a recorder be a director, so the AI side can never sign off its own work.

18
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:10,956
Step three, the dividend.

19
00:01:10,956 --> 00:01:18,044
One transaction pays all twenty holders pro-rata: one point six three million gas, about point one seven testnet MON.

20
00:01:18,044 --> 00:01:23,893
Before any transfer, the contract checks the holder list covers the whole share supply, with no duplicates.

21
00:01:24,742 --> 00:01:26,298
And here is the gate.

22
00:01:26,298 --> 00:01:30,838
We recorded a second update and never approved it, then tried to pay against it.

23
00:01:30,838 --> 00:01:36,463
BatchDividend asks UpdateAnchor itself, and the transaction reverts: update not approved.

24
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:37,867
No money moved.

25
00:01:38,718 --> 00:01:41,147
Anyone can check this without trusting us.

26
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:48,104
The page downloads the update, hashes it in the browser, and asks Monad directly whether a director approved exactly that content.

27
00:01:48,104 --> 00:01:48,989
Pass.

28
00:01:49,838 --> 00:01:51,017
Why Monad?

29
00:01:51,017 --> 00:01:54,826
Paying two hundred holders in one transaction costs about four cents.

30
00:01:54,826 --> 00:02:01,076
On Ethereum the same payout is about a hundred and seventy dollars at ten gwei, or two hundred separate claim transactions.

31
00:02:01,076 --> 00:02:05,533
And sub-second blocks mean the director sees the payout confirmed on the approval screen.

32
00:02:06,382 --> 00:02:13,799
Everything you have seen is live on Monad testnet: the contracts, every transaction, and each holder's balance, read straight from the chain.

33
00:02:13,799 --> 00:02:15,804
The holders are sample wallets.

34
00:02:15,804 --> 00:02:17,655
There are no real users yet.

35
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:19,960
Business Passport.

36
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:21,658
Know the business you own.

37
00:02:21,658 --> 00:02:22,932
Built on Monad.

38
00:02:22,932 --> 00:02:27,318
The code, the contracts and this demo are at monad dot blockid dot au.
